      "text": "We fix the problem on a cartesian plane with A at (0, 0), B at (8, 0), and C at (x, y). Now we use the Law of Cosines to solve for x and y:\n$AB^2 = AC^2 + BC^2 - 2(AC)(BC)\\cos{\\angle ABC}$\n$8^2 = 6^2 + 7^2 - 2(6)(7)\\cos{\\angle ABC}$\n$64 = 36 + 49 - 84\\cos{\\angle ABC}$\n$64 = 85 - 84\\cos{\\angle ABC}$\n$-21 = -84\\cos{\\angle ABC}$\n$\\cos{\\angle ABC} = \\frac{1}{4}$\nBy Law of Sines, $\\frac{BC}{\\sin{\\angle ABC}} = \\frac{AB}{\\sin{\\angle ACB}}$\n$\\frac{7}{\\sin{\\angle ABC}} = \\frac{8}{\\sin{\\angle ACB}}$\n$\\sin{\\angle ACB} = \\frac{8\\sin{\\angle ABC}}{7}$\n$\\sin{\\angle ACB} = \\frac{8 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}}{7}$\n$\\sin{\\angle ACB} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{15}}{7}$\nUsing the identity $\\sin^2{\\theta} + \\cos^2{\\theta} = 1$, we can solve for $\\sin{\\angle BAC}:$\n$\\sin^2{\\angle BAC} + \\cos^2{\\angle BAC} = 1$\n$\\sin^2{\\angle BAC} + \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^2 = 1$\n$\\sin^2{\\angle BAC} = \\frac{15}{16}$\n$\\sin{\\angle BAC} = \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}$\n$\\frac{PC}{\\sin \\angle ABC} = \\frac{AB}{\\sin \\angle ACB}$\n$\\frac{PC}{\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}} = \\frac{8}{\\frac{2\\sqrt{15}}{7}}$\n$PC = \\frac{8\\sqrt{15}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{15}}$\n$PC = \\frac{8 \\cdot 7}{8} = \\boxed{7}$",
